1
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:07,433
[engine starts]

2
00:00:12,679 --> 00:00:16,816
[Jerry] This is a 1970 
Mercedes 280 SL in signal red.

3
00:00:19,386 --> 00:00:23,423
It has a 170-horsepower, 
straight 6 engine, 2.8 liters.

4
00:00:24,700 --> 00:00:27,400
This was a car
for guys that want a sports car,

5
00:00:27,460 --> 00:00:29,429
but don't really 
wanna be inconvenienced.

6
00:00:29,496 --> 00:00:31,564
It's an "I get what 
the young people are doing

7
00:00:31,631 --> 00:00:33,033
and I'm part of it,

8
00:00:33,099 --> 00:00:35,769
but I will not be putting my mattress
on the floor."

9
00:00:35,835 --> 00:00:37,437
[line rings]

10
00:00:37,504 --> 00:00:39,339
Hello, Alec? It's Jerry.

11
00:00:39,433 --> 00:00:41,400
[Alec] Hey. How are you, man? 
What's going on?

12
00:00:41,466 --> 00:00:42,900
[Jerry]
Do you wanna grab a coffee?

13
00:00:42,942 --> 00:00:44,010
[Alec] Right now?

14
00:00:44,110 --> 00:00:45,211
[Jerry] Yeah, I'll pick you up.

15
00:00:45,300 --> 00:00:46,766
[Alec] I'll be downstairs 
in what, 30 minutes?

16
00:00:46,866 --> 00:00:47,866
[Jerry] Great.

17
00:00:55,889 --> 00:00:57,323
I'm Jerry Seinfeld

18
00:00:57,390 --> 00:00:59,893
and this is
Comedians in Cars getting Coffee.

19
00:01:01,666 --> 00:01:03,366
Today my guest is Alec Baldwin.

20
00:01:03,433 --> 00:01:05,066
Brilliant actor, movie star

21
00:01:05,133 --> 00:01:07,433
and one of my all time 
favorite funny people.

22
00:01:10,870 --> 00:01:12,138
You look good.

23
00:01:14,100 --> 00:01:15,133
Are you surprised?

24
00:01:21,966 --> 00:01:23,000
[Jerry] Look at that Häagen-Dazs,

25
00:01:23,049 --> 00:01:24,150
-look at that just sitting there.
-Yeah.

26
00:01:24,666 --> 00:01:27,766
-That's a whole rack of Häagen-Dazs.
-That looks good.

27
00:01:27,821 --> 00:01:29,989
-I gave up ice cream.
-I know.

28
00:01:30,056 --> 00:01:31,825
You've never been fat
in your life, have you?

29
00:01:32,466 --> 00:01:33,566
Have you?

30
00:01:33,633 --> 00:01:37,233
Your life has been one unbroken boulevard
of green lights, hasn't it?

31
00:01:41,201 --> 00:01:43,470
[Alec] You're from my hometown.
We're from the same town.

32
00:01:43,566 --> 00:01:44,766
[Jerry] Right. Massapequa.

33
00:01:45,066 --> 00:01:48,933
Who do you think has worked harder
to get where they are? You or me?

34
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:52,433
I think I've worked harder than you
and not gotten as far, quite frankly.

35
00:01:52,500 --> 00:01:55,399
-That's how it works.
-What do you consider work?

36
00:01:55,466 --> 00:01:59,033
Not walking into a room and everybody
just falls down and worships you.

37
00:01:59,366 --> 00:02:00,566
Really?

38
00:02:01,199 --> 00:02:02,933
[chuckles] "Really?

39
00:02:04,457 --> 00:02:05,391
That?"

40
00:02:10,699 --> 00:02:13,233
-[Alec] Do you eat there?
-[Jerry] Not anymore.

41
00:02:13,799 --> 00:02:14,833
I have.

42
00:02:16,302 --> 00:02:18,071
I'm gonna steal that.

43
00:02:18,138 --> 00:02:21,107
Burt Lancaster.
I don't remember whose story it is.

44
00:02:21,200 --> 00:02:23,766
They came up to his house,
and he came to the door,

45
00:02:23,833 --> 00:02:26,033
and his first line was, "Muffin, Bob?"

46
00:02:26,880 --> 00:02:30,316
My favorite Burt Lancaster story was
when they went to Burt Lancaster's trailer

47
00:02:30,400 --> 00:02:32,233
on the movie Tough Guys with Kirk Douglas.

48
00:02:32,299 --> 00:02:34,900
And they had a stack of glossies
of the two of them to sign.

49
00:02:34,954 --> 00:02:37,724
And the kid knocks on the door,
and Burt Lancaster says, "Come in."

50
00:02:37,790 --> 00:02:40,693
And they come in with a pen,
and Burt Lancaster's gonna sign them

51
00:02:40,760 --> 00:02:43,496
and he goes... [imitating Lancaster]
"Never mind. I got my own pen."

52
00:02:44,133 --> 00:02:48,966
And he takes out a big, thick Sharpie
and signs "Burt Lancaster" on the picture.

53
00:02:49,033 --> 00:02:51,033
He signs a stack of them, and he leaves.

54
00:02:51,100 --> 00:02:52,666
-They go to Kirk Douglas' trailer.
-[knocks]

55
00:02:52,733 --> 00:02:55,600
They knock on the door.
He says, "Come in."

56
00:02:55,666 --> 00:02:58,700
They open the door, he comes in
with a stack, they hand him the pen.

57
00:02:58,745 --> 00:03:01,347
And he writes it and his signature's
so puny next to it, he goes,

58
00:03:01,414 --> 00:03:03,650
[imitating Douglas]
"Can't you give me the pen that he had?"

59
00:03:03,716 --> 00:03:06,853
They go back to Burt Lancaster's trailer
and they knock on the door,

60
00:03:06,933 --> 00:03:09,399
and Burt Lancaster comes with the pen
in his hand and goes,

61
00:03:09,455 --> 00:03:11,558
-"I suppose you're looking for this."
-[chuckles]

62
00:03:13,766 --> 00:03:16,799
The two of them just [bleep]
with each other on every level.

63
00:03:17,633 --> 00:03:19,399
[imitating Lancaster]
"I suppose you're looking for this."

64
00:03:22,602 --> 00:03:27,407
[Jerry] What was the TV commercial
that you spent months trying to find?

65
00:03:27,500 --> 00:03:32,100
I tried to find the voice of a man
who was...

66
00:03:34,166 --> 00:03:35,700
He was a booth announcer.

67
00:03:36,200 --> 00:03:39,466
Back in the old days when I was a kid.
They had booth announcers.

68
00:03:39,533 --> 00:03:42,200
That way, if all that equipment,
if something got gummed up

69
00:03:42,266 --> 00:03:44,066
and they had a ma who'd be like...
[clears throat]

70
00:03:44,133 --> 00:03:48,766
"We are expecting technical difficulties.
We'll be back in a moment."

71
00:03:48,833 --> 00:03:51,533
The booth announcer I loved
was a guy named Robert Molly.

72
00:03:52,065 --> 00:03:55,468
In the end of Million Dollar Movie,
Robert Molly would come on

73
00:03:55,566 --> 00:03:57,833
to tell you what tomorrow's
million dollar movie would be.

74
00:03:57,899 --> 00:04:00,933
And he'd say... [imitating Molly]
"Barbara Stanwyck gives

75
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:03,733
Kirk Douglas a piece of her mind tomorrow

76
00:04:03,799 --> 00:04:05,566
on Million Dollar Movie."

77
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:07,833
And that was the voice of my childhood.

78
00:04:07,899 --> 00:04:09,633
And you wanted us to hear that again?

79
00:04:09,700 --> 00:04:12,600
I found it. Took me six months,
and I found it.

80
00:04:13,086 --> 00:04:16,055
We'll be back in a moment with
The Joe Franklin Show.

81
00:04:16,122 --> 00:04:17,657
[Jerry laughs]

82
00:04:19,866 --> 00:04:22,233
Have you seen the poster
for the new Three Stooges?

83
00:04:22,300 --> 00:04:23,266
The Three Stooges was something

84
00:04:23,333 --> 00:04:25,333
that even I could only take
in little spurts.

85
00:04:26,099 --> 00:04:27,233
Were you a Three Stooges fan?

86
00:04:27,300 --> 00:04:28,700
-I wasn't.
-I wasn't, either.

87
00:04:28,766 --> 00:04:30,800
Moe made me uncomfortable.

88
00:04:31,133 --> 00:04:34,099
Whenever someone would strike someone
with a metallic object.

89
00:04:34,166 --> 00:04:36,099
Like you hit someone
with a ball-pein hammer...

90
00:04:36,376 --> 00:04:38,011
-Yeah.
-...you lost-- I turn the channel.

91
00:04:41,833 --> 00:04:43,466
Do you run the garage?

92
00:04:44,566 --> 00:04:46,066
Do you guys have a coffee shop here?

93
00:04:46,633 --> 00:04:47,933
-[Jerry chuckles]
-No?

94
00:05:01,501 --> 00:05:03,503
-Coffee.
-I think I'll have a cappuccino.

95
00:05:03,569 --> 00:05:04,437
[waitress] Okay.

96
00:05:07,900 --> 00:05:10,666
I'd like a tuna salad sandwich
on some rye toast.

97
00:05:10,733 --> 00:05:12,566
I have a seven-grain, is that okay?

98
00:05:12,633 --> 00:05:14,033
That's the only kind of bread you have?

99
00:05:14,099 --> 00:05:15,833
-Seven grain.
-You only have seven-grain?

100
00:05:15,900 --> 00:05:17,500
[indistinct]

101
00:05:17,550 --> 00:05:20,053
We're not gonna get into a Jack Nicholson,
Five Easy Pieces kind of thing here,

102
00:05:20,119 --> 00:05:20,987
-are we?
-[Jerry chuckles]

103
00:05:21,800 --> 00:05:24,066
Let's start all over again.
What do you have?

104
00:05:24,657 --> 00:05:25,958
-Seven grain--
-That sounds perfect.

105
00:05:27,733 --> 00:05:29,366
[waitress] Do you want it toasted?

106
00:05:29,433 --> 00:05:31,666
[imitates Jack Nicholson]
"I want chicken salad."

107
00:05:31,733 --> 00:05:33,400
[waitress] What do you want?
We'll give you what we have.

108
00:05:33,466 --> 00:05:37,200
"What do you want? We'll give you
what we have." That's brilliant.

109
00:05:41,099 --> 00:05:44,000
I'm gonna be 54-years-old
in about three weeks.

110
00:05:44,066 --> 00:05:47,000
And I literally now
wake up every day and say to myself,

111
00:05:47,066 --> 00:05:48,900
"What do I wanna do
with the time I have left?"

112
00:05:48,966 --> 00:05:51,333
-Yeah.
-That list of "I have to do this

113
00:05:51,400 --> 00:05:54,000
"and I have to do this in my life"
is getting shorter.

114
00:05:54,066 --> 00:05:55,900
-Right.
-But the stuff that's on that list,

115
00:05:55,955 --> 00:05:57,490
-I really do have to do those things.
-Right.

116
00:05:57,557 --> 00:05:59,926
We were sitting on my back patio one time
and you said,

117
00:06:00,066 --> 00:06:04,533
"If I had a joint like this, I would set
up a phone right here and just go..."

118
00:06:04,599 --> 00:06:06,099
"No. No."

119
00:06:06,466 --> 00:06:08,766
When was the first time you got up
in front of an audience and performed?

120
00:06:08,833 --> 00:06:10,866
-I was 21-years-old.
-What place?

121
00:06:11,471 --> 00:06:14,507
Catch a Rising Star, 1st Avenue
and 78th. You must've gone there a lot.

122
00:06:14,599 --> 00:06:15,599
-I know Catch.
-Right.

123
00:06:15,666 --> 00:06:18,566
But there was nothing more amazing to me.
There was nothing.

124
00:06:18,633 --> 00:06:22,333
I was transfixed
by open mic comedy night

125
00:06:22,400 --> 00:06:23,466
-at Good Times.
-Really?

126
00:06:23,533 --> 00:06:26,900
You never wanted to be a straight actor?
Just legit acting?

127
00:06:27,700 --> 00:06:29,033
Were you ever tempted during your career?

128
00:06:29,088 --> 00:06:30,423
Did you ever sit there and go,
"I wanna try..."

129
00:06:30,490 --> 00:06:33,393
-Well, I did act.
-That was a good reading, by the way.

130
00:06:33,466 --> 00:06:36,166
-What?
-Good reading. "Well, I did act."

131
00:06:37,400 --> 00:06:39,000
I mean, wow.

132
00:06:39,065 --> 00:06:40,500
-I have five different awards.
-Do you?

133
00:06:40,566 --> 00:06:44,003
They're not from organizations
you'll recognize, but I have them.

134
00:06:44,099 --> 00:06:45,566
The American Association of Retailers.

135
00:06:46,033 --> 00:06:47,066
I've said this to you before,

136
00:06:47,133 --> 00:06:50,500
that your great gift
is the rapier-like comic skill...

137
00:06:50,566 --> 00:06:52,766
-What was that word?
-Rapier-like.

138
00:06:52,833 --> 00:06:56,066
R-a-p-i-e-r, hyphen, l-i--

139
00:06:56,133 --> 00:06:58,866
-Oh, rapier.
-Rapier. I say "rapier."

140
00:06:58,933 --> 00:07:01,533
-No? "Rapier?"
-That's really pretentious or wrong.

141
00:07:01,599 --> 00:07:04,133
Rapier could get you in trouble.
It could be misconstrued.

142
00:07:04,200 --> 00:07:07,966
-Rapier-like skills.
-Rapier is two rapists talking.

143
00:07:08,033 --> 00:07:09,866
-Yeah, exactly.
-"You think you're rapier than me?"

144
00:07:09,929 --> 00:07:12,031
-Right, exactly. "You don't know rapier."
-No...

145
00:07:12,098 --> 00:07:15,902
"My cellmate at Rahway,
he was the rapiest guy."

146
00:07:18,233 --> 00:07:19,766
[waitress] A tuna fish, not on rye.

147
00:07:19,833 --> 00:07:21,733
[Alec]
"A tuna fish, not on rye." You're funny.

148
00:07:21,799 --> 00:07:23,200
You know what I need from you,
if you don't mind?

149
00:07:24,110 --> 00:07:27,413
-May I have a fork and some napkins?
-Absolutely.

150
00:07:27,480 --> 00:07:29,615
-Do you have any pickles?
-[waitress] We have pickles.

151
00:07:29,700 --> 00:07:30,966
Could we get a bowl of pickles?

152
00:07:31,466 --> 00:07:33,766
-That was--
-I wanna get a bowl of pickles.

153
00:07:35,054 --> 00:07:36,556
And I wanna have a fork.

154
00:07:36,622 --> 00:07:40,693
I'd like some kind of dinnerware here,

155
00:07:40,760 --> 00:07:41,894
flatware.

156
00:07:42,528 --> 00:07:44,030
Is that gonna be a problem?

157
00:07:45,799 --> 00:07:47,433
Do you want me to start crying?

158
00:07:50,933 --> 00:07:52,666
So, you wanna talk about
this acting thing?

159
00:07:52,733 --> 00:07:54,066
But I wanna talk about why...

160
00:07:54,106 --> 00:07:55,641
First of all, you're looking at me,

161
00:07:55,708 --> 00:07:58,411
you know me well enough,
you can look in my eyes,

162
00:07:58,500 --> 00:08:00,366
you can see it's not there.

163
00:08:01,233 --> 00:08:02,533
You're not looking at me and going,

164
00:08:02,599 --> 00:08:04,933
"You know, you have something
that hasn't been tapped."

165
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:05,866
That's not true.

166
00:08:05,933 --> 00:08:08,666
You're looking at me thinking,
"Whatever you have has been used."

167
00:08:08,733 --> 00:08:10,233
-No, that's not true.
-Okay, fine.

168
00:08:10,289 --> 00:08:12,892
I look at you and I say,
"He's a lazy bastard.

169
00:08:14,060 --> 00:08:16,963
"He has everything in the world
he could possibly want.

170
00:08:17,029 --> 00:08:20,032
"And he's just a lazy, shiftless,
no-good bastard."

171
00:08:20,099 --> 00:08:21,299
-That's not true.
-Yeah.

172
00:08:21,366 --> 00:08:23,766
What are you asking me about?
Doing serious stuff?

173
00:08:23,803 --> 00:08:26,672
Do I have the urge to do
something straight, dramatic?

174
00:08:26,739 --> 00:08:28,641
I'm considered one
of the most articulate people

175
00:08:28,733 --> 00:08:30,733
in the Screen Actors Guild today.

176
00:08:30,799 --> 00:08:32,866
You seem to be having trouble
understanding what I'm saying.

177
00:08:32,933 --> 00:08:35,033
-Why do you think that is?
-You're looking at me,

178
00:08:35,100 --> 00:08:36,500
"This is a kid from Massapequa."

179
00:08:37,049 --> 00:08:38,484
A kid from Bar Harbor.

180
00:08:39,433 --> 00:08:40,833
-You remember the Slip'N Slide?
-Yes.

181
00:08:41,166 --> 00:08:44,566
You know, the Slip'n Slide, you run,
and then you stop running

182
00:08:44,633 --> 00:08:46,566
and continue moving forward.

183
00:08:46,933 --> 00:08:48,933
When I was a kid, that blew my mind.

184
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:50,866
In my neighborhood,
we were staring over our fences,

185
00:08:50,930 --> 00:08:52,431
staring at other people's Slip'n Slides.

186
00:08:53,033 --> 00:08:56,400
"That [bleep] Jerry got a [bleep]
Slip'n Slide! That hard-on.

187
00:08:56,466 --> 00:08:59,100
Look at that Slip'n Slide.
Can you imagine?

188
00:09:00,239 --> 00:09:01,274
"Jeez."

189
00:09:01,340 --> 00:09:02,508
"Must be nice."

190
00:09:02,600 --> 00:09:04,800
"Is anybody out here using it?
Anybody playing it?

191
00:09:05,433 --> 00:09:07,766
He's in the house. What's he doing?
Look through the window.

192
00:09:07,833 --> 00:09:10,133
He's talking into a hairbrush
in his bathroom."

193
00:09:10,199 --> 00:09:11,633
[Jerry laughing]

194
00:09:14,854 --> 00:09:16,022
I remember when I was a kid,

195
00:09:16,100 --> 00:09:19,000
my brothers would play wiffle ball
in the driveway, and I worked.

196
00:09:19,091 --> 00:09:22,028
Now, I would leave my house at ten o'clock
in the morning in the summertime.

197
00:09:22,094 --> 00:09:25,164
Is the picture you're painting for me here
that it was tough economically?

198
00:09:25,231 --> 00:09:28,000
No, it was gonna lead me up
to what trash my brothers were.

199
00:09:28,066 --> 00:09:30,633
'Cause they would be playing wiffle ball
in the driveway at ten a.m.,

200
00:09:30,699 --> 00:09:32,500
and when I came back
at four o'clock in the afternoon,

201
00:09:32,566 --> 00:09:34,100
they were still playing
the same game of wiffle ball.

202
00:09:34,140 --> 00:09:36,842
They played wiffle ball
for six hours in the [bleep] driveway.

203
00:09:36,909 --> 00:09:38,711
So, you were the more responsible one.

204
00:09:38,778 --> 00:09:41,213
I was Abraham Lincoln to them.

205
00:09:41,300 --> 00:09:44,133
Yeah. And they were all John Wilkes Booth.

206
00:09:49,266 --> 00:09:51,166
What's it like being a dad for you?

207
00:09:51,557 --> 00:09:55,695
Being a dad is, is, you know,
it's a surfboard on a rainbow.

208
00:09:56,696 --> 00:10:00,900
You're getting all you can handle of life.

209
00:10:00,967 --> 00:10:03,302
What I like about having kids
is when they're on the toilet

210
00:10:03,369 --> 00:10:05,204
and you knock on the door,
they just go, "Come in."

211
00:10:05,905 --> 00:10:07,073
Nobody else does that.

212
00:10:07,139 --> 00:10:10,109
Nobody just invites you in
while they're on the toilet...

213
00:10:10,776 --> 00:10:12,078
-defecating.
-Yeah.

214
00:10:15,900 --> 00:10:19,333
The demographic for the Academy is
very, very interesting.

215
00:10:19,400 --> 00:10:21,466
I went there, and I hosted the Oscars,

216
00:10:21,533 --> 00:10:23,966
and you'd have people walk up to you,
it is very touching.

217
00:10:24,033 --> 00:10:26,433
I was moved, and a guy
would walk up to you that was...

218
00:10:26,966 --> 00:10:28,100
80-years-old.

219
00:10:28,166 --> 00:10:30,633
This guy went to shake my hand,
he goes,

220
00:10:30,699 --> 00:10:31,933
[imitating man] "I'm Bob Tucker,

221
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:36,300
I was a stunt coordinator on
She Wore a Yellow Ribbon.

222
00:10:36,366 --> 00:10:38,866
And I just wanna say
thank you very much for doing this.

223
00:10:38,933 --> 00:10:41,666
It means the world to us that
you're taking the time out of your--"

224
00:10:41,733 --> 00:10:42,866
Cut to the next woman...

225
00:10:42,933 --> 00:10:44,366
[imitating woman] "I'm Muriel Wittingham.

226
00:10:45,066 --> 00:10:47,500
I was the art director on Vertigo."

227
00:10:47,566 --> 00:10:49,533
And you go, " Oh, my God".

228
00:10:49,600 --> 00:10:54,066
You're gonna get to change the whole thing
from the bottom up. What would you do?

229
00:10:54,620 --> 00:10:56,489
What would the better Oscar show be?

230
00:10:56,566 --> 00:10:57,800
It would be two-hours long.

231
00:10:58,433 --> 00:11:01,000
And you'd have a lot
of reverence for film,

232
00:11:01,060 --> 00:11:03,262
and you'd have it be much more serious.

233
00:11:03,329 --> 00:11:05,998
-You think that would work?
-No. That's the problem.

234
00:11:06,065 --> 00:11:07,533
-Would you ever host the Oscars?
-No.

235
00:11:07,633 --> 00:11:08,600
-You would never.
-No.

236
00:11:08,666 --> 00:11:10,866
-You wouldn't get near it?
-No.

237
00:11:14,300 --> 00:11:15,466
"Can I get more iced tea?"

238
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:17,266
"Hello, no."

239
00:11:17,543 --> 00:11:18,878
"Thank you, Roger."

240
00:11:19,300 --> 00:11:23,566
If you're producing and I can talk to you
during the broadcast,

241
00:11:23,633 --> 00:11:25,633
and you're off-camera there...

242
00:11:25,685 --> 00:11:27,186
-Like Fred de Cordova.
-Sure.

243
00:11:27,253 --> 00:11:28,721
-I'll be your Rip Torn.
-Yeah.

244
00:11:29,221 --> 00:11:32,825
Rip Torn did a part in Bee Movie with me.
I love that guy.

245
00:11:32,900 --> 00:11:34,800
He's doing the show with me and I go,
"Where do you live?"

246
00:11:34,860 --> 00:11:37,163
He goes... [imitates Rip]
"I live up there in Salisbury."

247
00:11:37,229 --> 00:11:38,998
I said, "What's it like where you live?"

248
00:11:39,065 --> 00:11:40,132
He literally said to me,

249
00:11:40,500 --> 00:11:42,800
[imitates Rip] "Some of the people
up there are a little strange.

250
00:11:42,900 --> 00:11:44,866
One time I was in a bar up there

251
00:11:44,933 --> 00:11:50,000
and these two guys are at the bar,
and they gave me the eye, see?

252
00:11:50,066 --> 00:11:51,633
And I knew something was gonna go down."

253
00:11:51,699 --> 00:11:53,900
I'm sitting there going,
"What? What happened?"

254
00:11:53,966 --> 00:11:56,266
He goes, "I started to go out the door
and go to the car,

255
00:11:56,315 --> 00:11:59,685
and the one guy makes a move on me,
so I crack him in the jaw.

256
00:11:59,752 --> 00:12:01,987
The other guy smashes me
with a bottle in the face.

257
00:12:02,066 --> 00:12:03,933
I kick the other guy in the balls.

258
00:12:03,989 --> 00:12:04,990
I stick a fork in the other..."

259
00:12:05,057 --> 00:12:08,627
He's describing this
Peckinpah-esque melee.

260
00:12:08,694 --> 00:12:10,429
My mouth is on the floor.
I go, "When did this happen?"

261
00:12:10,500 --> 00:12:11,933
He went...
[imitates Rip] "Last summer."

262
00:12:13,966 --> 00:12:17,036
You know, he's like 74. He is...

263
00:12:17,103 --> 00:12:19,672
-That's great.
-Yeah, he's crazy. He's crazy.

264
00:12:19,738 --> 00:12:22,942
This, by the way, is your curse.
And I say this about you all the time.

265
00:12:23,566 --> 00:12:28,266
You're a gifted, gifted actor,
who is cursed with the mind of a writer.

266
00:12:29,181 --> 00:12:32,017
I've gotten more, uh, into that

267
00:12:32,100 --> 00:12:34,233
as a result of being around
the people I've been around.

268
00:12:34,286 --> 00:12:37,790
I don't think I'm that funny,
but I'm totally comfortable when I say,

269
00:12:37,857 --> 00:12:40,025
"I learned a lot
from being around them for six years.

270
00:12:40,099 --> 00:12:41,599
They taught me a lot."

271
00:12:41,660 --> 00:12:43,562
What you know cannot be taught, my friend.

272
00:12:43,629 --> 00:12:46,532
-Well, in some ways, I appreciate that.
-Just take the compliment.

273
00:12:46,599 --> 00:12:50,000
You realize we have to leave Rebecca
a $1,000 tip? Do you know that?

274
00:12:50,066 --> 00:12:51,233
This is the situation we painted.

275
00:12:55,474 --> 00:12:57,576
-I moved downtown. It's very strange.
-[Jerry] Tell me how it's going.

276
00:12:57,643 --> 00:12:59,044
Oh, it's so strange.

277
00:12:59,111 --> 00:13:01,480
In your apartment,
at three o'clock in the morning, you hear,

278
00:13:02,266 --> 00:13:06,533
[imitates woman] "[bleep] you, Steven!
Steven! Get back here!"

279
00:13:08,099 --> 00:13:10,233
Like a full five minutes go by
and then it's,

280
00:13:11,400 --> 00:13:13,099
[imitates woman]
"[bleep] you, Steven!"

281
00:13:13,159 --> 00:13:14,894
I'm like,
"She's still at [bleep] you, Steven."

282
00:13:14,960 --> 00:13:17,763
-Like it's a pot that's just boiling.
-That's the energy of youth.

283
00:13:17,866 --> 00:13:19,266
Only youth can give you that.

284
00:13:19,333 --> 00:13:21,333
Only young people
can keep those flames burning,

285
00:13:21,400 --> 00:13:23,135
those pots boiling all that time.

286
00:13:25,866 --> 00:13:27,333
[Alec imitating woman]
"[bleep] you, Steven!

287
00:13:28,366 --> 00:13:31,300
Steven, get back here!"

